| maximum word length | cumulative actual words | distinct native forms | distinct permutation actions | mismatches |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 0 |
| 1 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 0 |
| 2 | 7 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 3 | 15 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 4 | 31 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 5 | 63 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 6 | 127 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 7 | 255 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 8 | 511 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 9 | 1023 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
| 10 | 2047 | 6 | 6 | 0 |
\image(figures/knuth_bendix_census.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A complete finite word census checked against independently composed permutations.)
\alttext(All enumerated words are checked for action preservation and unique native representative per action; the six-action line is an explicit group-model reference.)

> Caps and nonconfluence are explicit limits

The positive retained-rule ceiling is checked before adding both initial oriented equations and newly discovered critical equations. An initial cap can leave later input equations unprocessed. The fixed2000-round guard also returns complete=false. These are not limits on total fork bytes, reductions, elapsed time or peak memory. An incomplete result supplies only sound consequences of the processed equations: equal reductions imply equality, while unequal reductions need not imply inequality.

The controls compare several complete rule ceilings on the original presentation. A separate valid but nonconfluent pair ab→a, ab→b shows why deterministic first-rule reduction is not by itself canonical. Invalid empty-lhs, identity or increasing rules are rejected before reduction, including unused rules, rather than permitting an infinite unchecked loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/knuth_bendix_caps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/knuth_bendix.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> x, y;
  std::print("| actual rule ceiling | returned rules | complete |\n| ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
  for (int cap : {1, 2, 3, 4, 8, 16, 64}) {
    const auto c = dm::algorithms::knuth_bendix({{"aa", ""}, {"bbb", ""}, {"abab", ""}}, cap);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", cap, c.rules.size(), c.complete);
    x.push_back(cap);
    y.push_back(c.rules.size());
  }
  std::print("\n| valid nonconfluent rule order | actual reduction of ab |\n| --- | --- |\n");
  std::print(
      "| ab to a first | {} |\n", dm::algorithms::kb_normal_form("ab", {{"ab", "a"}, {"ab", "b"}}));
  std::print(
      "| ab to b first | {} |\n", dm::algorithms::kb_normal_form("ab", {{"ab", "b"}, {"ab", "a"}}));
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(x, y, "rules", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(x, y, "", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .y_limits(-.5, 10)
      .title("Actual capped completion outcomes")
      .x_label("complete retained-rule ceiling")
      .y_label("actual returned rule count")
      .save_svg("build/knuth_bendix_caps.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual rule ceiling | returned rules | complete |
| ---: | ---: | --- |
| 1 | 1 | false |
| 2 | 2 | false |
| 3 | 3 | false |
| 4 | 4 | false |
| 8 | 6 | true |
| 16 | 6 | true |
| 64 | 6 | true |

| valid nonconfluent rule order | actual reduction of ab |
| --- | --- |
| ab to a first | a |
| ab to b first | b |
\image(figures/knuth_bendix_caps.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Each rule ceiling is a separate actual completion run; status is reported explicitly.)
\alttext(Returned rule counts stay within the complete ceiling; the table distinguishes completed scans from incomplete exits and demonstrates order dependence for nonconfluent rules.)

> Scope and verification

Words and rules are arbitrary byte strings with identity represented by empty string. A caller-supplied reducer system must strictly decrease shortlex; this guarantees termination but not confluence. Complete input rules are checked before copies. Traces own the original word/rules and actual before/after values. The million-entry default counts one initial-word record, each rule and each application; zero disables only this optional quota. Full word bytes within records, private copies and capacities are excluded, so the quota does not bound peak memory. Each application is admitted before replacement and no partial trace escapes failure.

An independent permutation model verified all2047 words through length10 and7315 actual replacements for the original S3 presentation. Independently overlaid critical forks joined under the final rules, and actual steps respected first-rule/leftmost choice and strict shortlex descent. Frozen checks preserved2044 word reductions on four original completion systems. Native controls verify equal-length progress, nonconfluence, complete rule ceilings, invalid unused-rule rejection and ownership. The ten-report shared build passed141 native CTest cases,45 directly instrumented address/undefined-behaviour sanitizer cases,90 compiled/executed public examples,412/412 scoped file definitions plus11/11 changed lagged-Fibonacci engine definitions, and ten representative clangd hovers. All four complete report programs executed and all four native figures were visually reviewed. Receipts and repeatable checks are retained under `build/mepml-conversion/batch-184-193/`.

> References

D. E. Knuth and P. B. Bendix(1970), [Simple Word Problems in Universal Algebras|https://doi.org/10.1016/B978-0-08-012975-4.50028-X], in Computational Problems in Abstract Algebra,263–297. This report specializes completion to finite byte strings, shortlex order and the actual native first-rule/leftmost strategy.
